course: dictionary
lesson: 5301
title: Two meanings, one word
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the clues around them tell you which one the writer means. A history book might say «Kublai Khan's fleets attacked Japan in 1274 and 1281 and failed, partly because of storms the Japanese called kamikaze, divine wind.» The words divine wind are the clue: kamikaze is the Japanese name for those storms, and it means divine wind.
explain: Kamikaze also has a sad history in the Second World War. Japanese pilots flew planes full of explosives into enemy ships on purpose, knowing they would die in the attack. History lessons use this meaning, so look at the nearby words about war, planes and ships to know which meaning fits.
explain: In everyday talk, kamikaze is an informal word. If someone says, «That was a kamikaze run down the hill», they mean the rider went wildly fast and reckless, as if they did not care about getting hurt. Check your choice by putting reckless into the sentence: if it still makes sense, you have the right meaning.
fact: Kamikaze is a Japanese word meaning divine wind.
fact: In the Second World War, a kamikaze was a Japanese pilot who flew a plane full of explosives into an enemy ship on purpose.
fact: «Kamikaze» can also describe something wildly reckless, as in «a kamikaze run down the hill».
fact: Words from history lessons can have a different meaning in everyday talk, so always check the clues around the word.
fact: Kamikaze can be a noun or an adjective; the plural of the noun is kamikazes.
exercise: «Kublai Khan's fleets failed partly because of storms the Japanese called kamikaze, divine wind.» What does kamikaze mean in that sentence? | A kind of Japanese food | A big ship full of explosives | A quiet, gentle breeze | *Divine wind
exercise: «The cyclist made a kamikaze run down the hill.» What does kamikaze suggest here? | Quiet and kind | Calm and sleepy | Very slow and careful | *Wildly reckless, as if not caring about getting hurt
exercise: In the Second World War, what was a kamikaze? | A type of Japanese rice cake | A kind of storm that always comes in winter | *A Japanese pilot who flew a plane full of explosives into an enemy ship on purpose | A Japanese ship that sailed around the world
test: Kamikaze is a Japanese word. What does it mean in English? | *Divine wind | Brave heart | Lightning strike | Gentle rain
test: Which clue shows that Ruth's kamikaze dive was reckless in «Ruth's kamikaze dive off the rocks was wild and careless»? | Ruth is a name | *Wild and careless | Rocks are hard | Dive is a word
exam: Read: «The pilot's kamikaze mission was in the war.» Which meaning fits, and why? | The reckless meaning, because a mission is always a run down a hill | The meaning short and rude | The divine wind meaning, because a pilot is a kind of storm | *The Second World War meaning, because a pilot, a mission and a war all go together
